Legal systems operate on the assumption that individuals are responsible for their actions, which is only coherent if they possess free will.
Moral judgments require the belief that people have the power to choose their behavior, as without this, blame and praise lose their meaning.
Without free will, the concept of justice and ethical behavior becomes meaningless, as it would be impossible to hold anyone accountable for their actions.
If free will did not exist, then all human behavior would be determined, making moral responsibility an illusion and ethical systems obsolete.
The idea that people can choose their actions is central to personal identity and autonomy, which are foundational to ethical and legal frameworks.